#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f43359-c122-54be-8d77-4bb4267b5859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286365} {:answer/id #uuid "723ef586-19db-5f1f-9401-00c82807fbc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286366} {:answer/id #uuid "b168ca86-8ccb-5432-8645-a79cad4f817e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286367} {:answer/id #uuid "917a34d0-4e26-59b9-b37c-dcd906042c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286368} {:answer/id #uuid "15eab90f-310c-5d6f-83fd-1ec9c2cf5af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "917a34d0-4e26-59b9-b37c-dcd906042c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286368} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc81dd60-8bf1-5bcd-ba31-07b1c6bdffb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e11c8036-c7f9-5f36-af42-ea8dbcd9d81a" :question/math-academy-id "q-14680" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}=8.$ The tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1)$ on the curve intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1).$  \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4x+12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4x}{12y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{x}{3y})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(0-1)+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f9d0166-6bec-5851-bb1c-43edcbc681ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286371} {:answer/id #uuid "22385bbd-e241-5b75-8da1-6b738f584e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286372} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeb15bd-6c98-5c6b-840a-b4a90fd4c2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286373} {:answer/id #uuid "9457067f-ebde-554e-aab5-df5dc4c93e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286374} {:answer/id #uuid "c301127b-8a8e-5745-b3ec-63b4d2a16958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(2y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9d0166-6bec-5851-bb1c-43edcbc681ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286371} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b3a4713-a4ec-5580-9fe2-79c0bbc9a91a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f5ae45c-0d29-593f-abd0-85c3a45aaf13" :question/math-academy-id "q-2287" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\ [(x)(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))] & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a969d92a-1eba-5894-8ba0-1e6b6abddb74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286378} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e55992-6baa-5167-90d7-9d4211d45add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a969d92a-1eba-5894-8ba0-1e6b6abddb74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286378} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfc616a9-6fee-5cfb-ab2e-7831a33a9f72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f462b566-6d04-56f6-a1d4-dfc5ad7d21ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-312258" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y=6x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ [3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}]-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ (3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4})}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d5ad37-a317-56c6-99a2-d30b41fd0a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5d5ad37-a317-56c6-99a2-d30b41fd0a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286382} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d53ad5-4ec5-5e16-8919-1fc595be6b13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f6ce041-9abc-5780-ad8c-e607d68b1ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f6ce041-9abc-5780-ad8c-e607d68b1ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286384} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d0b1c5-893e-58d6-ab09-47e4d09ba084" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "469198e0-64e7-505b-94c7-39658b9e1be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-342401" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/assets/0c89b1d28ce7692f54e0fbf32c1d99b091e24729c837883523d6150450decb17.png)\n\nThe equation $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=20x$ represents an ellipse. The tangent line at the point $(4,4)$ on the ellipse intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(4,4).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) \\\\ 8x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20-8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{20-8x}{2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{10-4x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{10-4x}{y})|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-4\\cdot 4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-4)+4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 0+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,10).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fcabb71-ad83-52a9-a13b-b93a38b6f87b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286387} {:answer/id #uuid "00cc0209-6ebb-5b89-a54e-9c76fdb709b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286388} {:answer/id #uuid "c71f133a-6cfd-5ef9-9da5-3887ffa064bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286389} {:answer/id #uuid "a06e5a4a-9bbd-58a2-bc94-958d8e84d3da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286390} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8f6020-b125-5c3f-a2ee-bcb4e811fb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{y}-2x}{x\\,{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c71f133a-6cfd-5ef9-9da5-3887ffa064bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286389} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9f0599-bd68-55b1-a398-49fae9ea009f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82be5ee4-1296-57ae-8b02-9dee15c642bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-72214" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x{e}^{y}={x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x{e}^{y}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ [(x)({e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+(1)({e}^{y})] & =2x \\\\ x\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x \\\\ x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-{e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
